    "summary": "The National Anti-Money Laundering Committee has added 10 individuals, including former Southwest State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed (Laftagareen), to its terrorism financing sanctions list.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, October 13, 2026 — The National Anti-Money Laundering and Combating Terrorism Financing Committee (NAMLC) announced on Monday that it has added 10 individuals to its financial sanctions list, citing alleged links to terrorism financing. Among those listed is Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed (Laftagareen), former president of the Southwest State of Somalia. The list also includes former Southwest State Security Minister Hassan Abdulkadir Mohamed, former Custodial Corps Commander Mahad Abdirahman Adan (Shub), and Ibrahim Adan, among others.\n\nThe NAMLC, a government body responsible for implementing Somalia's anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism financing measures, stated that the individuals are subject to asset freezes and travel bans. The committee did not provide detailed evidence for each designation, but such listings are typically based on intelligence and financial investigations. This is the first time a former regional president has been publicly named in such a list, marking a significant escalation in Somalia's efforts to combat terrorism financing.\n\nLaftagareen served as president of Southwest State from 2018 until 2025, when he was replaced through a regional election. His tenure was marked by tensions with the federal government over political and security issues. The inclusion of high-profile figures like Laftagareen and former security officials suggests a broadening of accountability measures. The Somali federal government has intensified its campaign against al-Shabaab's financial networks in recent years, often targeting individuals accused of facilitating illicit funding.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly relevant. Many diaspora members send remittances to family and business partners in Southwest State and other regions. The sanctions could affect money transfer operators (MTOs) and banks that facilitate transactions involving the named individuals, potentially leading to increased scrutiny of transfers to Somalia. Diaspora organizations have long urged for transparency and due process in such listings to avoid disruptions to legitimate remittances, which are a lifeline for many Somali families.\n\nThe NAMLC's move may also impact political dynamics in Southwest State, where Laftagareen remains a influential figure. His supporters might view the sanctions as politically motivated, while the federal government maintains they are based on credible evidence. The lack of detailed public evidence could fuel debate over the fairness of the process. The Somali government has not yet issued a detailed statement beyond the NAMLC's announcement.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/13/madaxdii-hore-ee-koonfur-galbeed-oo-lagu-daray-liiska-cunaqabateynta-maaliyadeed/",
